%[text] # Carnot in Motion: A Time-Dependent Simulation with MATLAB
%[text] ***What happens when we introduce time into the classical Carnot cycle?***
%[text] Prof. Rodolfo Salazar, Instituto Tecnológico de Estudios Superiores de Monterrey - Campus Guadalajara, México. (`mailto:rodolfo.salazar@tec.mx`) 
%[text] **Abstract:** *Thermodynamics is often introduced through equilibrium states and state diagrams, which may lead students to perceive it as a predominantly static discipline. This activity explores a different perspective by introducing time explicitly into the classical Carnot cycle through MATLAB. An ideal gas confined in a piston-cylinder system undergoes the four reversible processes of the cycle, while its volume is defined as a periodic function of time. Pressure is determined throughout the process, allowing the system's evolution to be visualized through time-dependent volume and pressure plots, as well as the conventional pressure-volume diagram. Numerical integration is then used to calculate the work transferred throughout successive cycles, revealing its cumulative behavior over time. The resulting visualization connects thermodynamic states, process trajectories, and energy conversion in a single computational experience. Through simulation and guided questions, students can explore the relationship between operating conditions, work production, and Carnot efficiency, developing a more intuitive understanding of thermodynamic processes and the role of time in engineering systems.*
%[text] **Keywords:**  Carnot Cycle, Thermodynamics Education, Time-Dependent Simulation, Numerical Integration, MATLAB-Based Learning.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of contents"}
